course: dictionary
lesson: 5553
title: Lemonade and the words around it
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Lemonade is a noun, a word for a thing. Its meaning is a sweet drink made from lemon juice, water and sugar. The words around it, its context, tell you which kind of lemonade the writer means.
explain: In the UK, Ireland, Australia and New Zealand, lemonade often means a clear, sweet, fizzy drink. Look at «Raisins in lemonade rise when bubbles stick to them», and the bubbles tell you it is fizzy.
explain: A lemonade is one glass or bottle of it, so you can count lemonades: one lemonade, two lemonades. Context helps again. «The price matters too: at £2 a cup hardly anyone buys, at 50p lots do» shows that lemonade is often sold by the cup.
fact: Lemonade is a noun, and its plural is lemonades.
fact: Lemonade means a sweet drink made from lemon juice, water and sugar.
fact: In the UK, Ireland, Australia and New Zealand, lemonade often means a clear, sweet, fizzy drink.
fact: A lemonade is one glass or bottle of it.
fact: «When life gives you lemons, make lemonade» means make the best of a bad thing.
exercise: What is lemonade made from? | *Lemon juice, water and sugar | Milk and flour | Tea and honey | Salt and vinegar
exercise: «A boy in Colorado runs a lemonade stand with his dad.» What does a lemonade stand sell? | Fresh bread | Lemons only | *Cups of lemonade | Old toys
exercise: «at £2 a cup hardly anyone buys, at 50p lots do» Which price did lots of people buy at? | *50p a cup | £2 a cup | £5 a cup | £10 a cup
test: A stand sells 30 cups of lemonade at 50p each. How much money does it make? | £3 | £30 | £150 | *£15
test: The stand makes £15 and the lemons and sugar cost £5. What is the profit? | £15 | £5 | £20 | *£10
exam: In «Raisins in lemonade rise when bubbles stick to them», what does fizzy mean? | *Full of bubbles | Made with milk | Sour and bitter | Very cold
